Project NordicCart {
  database_type: 'PostgreSQL'
  Note: '''
# NordicCart operational database (OLTP source), revision 3
Omnichannel consumer electronics retailer in Sweden, Norway, Denmark and Finland. Synthetic data.
Source system only: no raw, staging or analytical warehouse tables.

## Legend used in all notes
- [DBML] enforced by the PostgreSQL DDL generated from this file (PK, FK, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, DEFAULT, CHECK, enum).
- [PG] must be added during PostgreSQL implementation because DBML cannot express it (partial unique index, EXCLUDE, trigger, sequence option, privilege, setting).
- [APP] application or simulator logic.
DBML enforces nothing by itself: a rule is real only once the generated DDL plus the [PG] items are applied.

## Conventions
- Surrogate keys are bigint identity values, never reused. Business keys (sku, customer_number, order_number, return_number, location_code, refund_reference) are unique and stable.
- Timestamps are timestamptz in UTC. Business event times (placed_at, fulfilled_at, occurred_at, ...) are separate from system times (created_at, updated_at).
- Every amount on an order and its child records is in orders.currency_code, VAT-inclusive unless the column says excl. VAT. Amounts are never converted or overwritten in place.

## Change data capture (details in change_log)
- Extraction reads change_log only, using a snapshot checkpoint (pg_current_snapshot / pg_visible_in_snapshot), never change_id ranges, updated_at or overlap windows.
- updated_at and created_at default to now(), the transaction START time, so a long transaction can commit rows stamped before the last watermark. They are for audit and reconciliation, not for incremental watermarks.

## Units of work (each is one PostgreSQL transaction, READ COMMITTED)
Lock order everywhere: orders row -> inventory_balances rows sorted by (stock_location_id, product_id) -> reservation and movement inserts in the same sorted order -> payment_attempts row. A fixed order prevents deadlocks.
- U1 Place: insert orders (placed) and order_items with the price snapshot. U1 and U2 may share one transaction.
- U2 Reserve (all lines or none): SELECT the order FOR UPDATE (status placed), SELECT its balance rows FOR UPDATE in lock order, check quantity_on_hand - quantity_reserved >= quantity for every line. If any line is short: ROLLBACK, then a new transaction sets the order cancelled (out_of_stock). Otherwise insert one active reservation per line and set the order stock_reserved.
- U3 Pay: (a) insert payment_attempts (pending) and commit. (b) Call the provider OUTSIDE any transaction with idempotency key order_number-attempt_number; it authorizes or fails. (c) Lock the order FOR UPDATE: if status is stock_reserved and all reservations are active, mark the attempt captured and the order confirmed; otherwise void the attempt. A failed attempt may be retried (new row) while reservations are active, maximum 3 attempts; after the last failure set the order payment_failed and release its reservations in the same transaction.
- U4 Expire: a sweeper locks due orders with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, one order per transaction: reservations -> expired, authorized attempts -> voided, order -> cancelled (reservation_expired).
- U5 Fulfil: lock the order (status confirmed) and its balance rows in lock order; per line FIRST set the reservation consumed, THEN insert the sale movement; finally set the order fulfilled. Any zero-row update aborts the whole transaction.
- U6 Restock return: insert one return_restock movement per resellable return item at returns.received_location_id.
- U7 Receipts, transfers, adjustments, write-offs: insert movements only. Stock that is reserved cannot be transferred or written off because the balance CHECK reserved <= on_hand would fail.

## Retry rules [APP]
- Retry the whole unit of work, up to 3 times with jittered backoff, on SQLSTATE 40001 (serialization failure), 40P01 (deadlock) and 55P03 (lock timeout).
- Never retry 23514 (CHECK violation) or 23503 (FK violation): they are business rejections, for example no stock.
- 23505 on an idempotency index (partial uniques below) means the step already happened: reload state and continue.
- Every status change is guarded (UPDATE ... WHERE status = expected), so a repeated step updates zero rows and is a no-op.
- Provider calls (payments, refunds) are never made while holding row locks and always carry an idempotency key. A timed-out call stays pending and is reconciled with the provider before any new attempt.

## Reporting rules for Power BI
Rounding: 2 decimals, round half away from zero (PostgreSQL round(numeric), DAX ROUND). The Python generator must use Decimal with ROUND_HALF_UP, never float or banker rounding.
Per order line (stored at purchase, checked by [DBML] CHECKs):
- gross_sales_incl_vat = quantity * unit_price
- discount_amount = round(quantity * unit_price * discount_percent / 100, 2)
- line_total (net sales incl. VAT) = gross_sales_incl_vat - discount_amount
- vat_amount = round(line_total * vat_rate / (1 + vat_rate), 2)
- net_revenue_excl_vat = line_total - vat_amount
- cogs = quantity * unit_cost (unit_cost excl. VAT)
- gross_profit = net_revenue_excl_vat - cogs; gross margin % = gross_profit / net_revenue_excl_vat
VAT is computed per line and summed upwards, never recomputed on order or report totals.
Shipping: shipping_fee incl. VAT, shipping_vat_amount = round(shipping_fee * vat_rate / (1 + vat_rate), 2) at the line VAT rate. Shipping income excl. VAT is reported separately from product revenue and carries no COGS.
Recognition: a sale counts only when the order status is fulfilled, dated by fulfilled_at converted to local time with countries.time_zone. Other statuses are funnel KPIs, not revenue.
Returns: dated by returns.closed_at of completed returns. Returned revenue excl. VAT = SUM(refund_amount - refund_vat_amount). Returned COGS = returned quantity * unit_cost for resellable items only; damaged items keep their cost as a loss.
Net figures: net revenue = sales revenue - returned revenue; COGS after returns = COGS - returned COGS; gross profit after returns = net revenue - COGS after returns.
Currency: never add amounts across currencies. EUR = local amount / exchange_rates.rate for the event date (sale: fulfilled date, return: closed date), converted at line grain without intermediate rounding; round only for display.

## Enforcement map
[DBML] generated: tables, enums, primary keys, all foreign keys (including the composite type-checked and in-store fulfilment keys), UNIQUE constraints and indexes, NOT NULL, defaults, and the named row-level CHECKs (money arithmetic, VAT and discount rounding, status-timestamp pairs, movement sign and reference rules, no oversell).
Caveats: CHECKs only see one row. Composite and nullable FKs use MATCH SIMPLE and are skipped when any column is NULL (intended for online orders). The >? notation is visual only; nullability comes from the column definition.
[PG] to add during implementation:
- Partial unique indexes: inventory_reservations(order_item_id) WHERE status = active; inventory_movements(order_item_id) WHERE movement_type = sale; inventory_movements(return_item_id) WHERE movement_type = return_restock; inventory_movements(related_movement_id) WHERE movement_type = transfer_in; payment_attempts(order_id) WHERE status IN (pending, authorized, captured); refunds(refund_reference) WHERE status IN (pending, succeeded).
- EXCLUDE on product_prices validity periods (btree_gist).
- Triggers: updated_at maintenance; change_log capture on every tracked table; insert-only guards; balance maintenance from movements and reservations; reservation consistency; order status transition matrix; deferred order total check; payment, shipment, return item and refund guards; deferred stock location subtype check.
- change_log identity with CACHE 1; column privileges (no UPDATE on balance quantities, no UPDATE or DELETE on ledgers); idle_in_transaction_session_timeout and lock_timeout.
[APP]: fulfilment location choice, promotion eligibility, provider calls and retry limits, reservation expiry scheduling, return window, refund creation timing, extraction job.

## MVP implementation plan (target ERD keeps all tables)
Phase 1, MVP (orders, reservations, payments, fulfilment): currencies, countries, exchange_rates, product_categories, products, product_prices, customers, stock_locations, stores, warehouses, inventory_balances, inventory_reservations, inventory_movements, orders, order_items, payment_attempts, change_log, change_log_checkpoints. Build change capture first so the pipeline can start from the bootstrap snapshot. In the MVP, order_items.promotion_id and inventory_movements.return_item_id stay NULL; their FKs are added with the tables they point to. Confirmed orders are always fulfilled (cancellation after payment needs refunds).
Phase 2, promotions: promotions, plus the order_items.promotion_id FK.
Phase 3, shipping: shipments.
Phase 4, after-sales: returns, return_items, refunds, plus the inventory_movements.return_item_id FK and their partial uniques and guards.
'''
}

// =====================================================================
// Enums
// =====================================================================

Enum stock_location_type {
  store
  warehouse
}

Enum sales_channel {
  online
  in_store
}

Enum order_status {
  placed [note: 'Order captured, stock not yet reserved']
  stock_reserved [note: 'All lines reserved at the fulfilment location, awaiting a captured payment']
  confirmed [note: 'Payment captured']
  fulfilled [note: 'Stock issued: picked and packed (online) or handed over (in store)']
  payment_failed [note: 'Terminal: last payment attempt failed, reservations released']
  cancelled [note: 'Terminal: out of stock, reservation expired or customer request']
}

Enum reservation_status {
  active
  consumed [note: 'Converted into a sale movement at fulfilment']
  released [note: 'Released after cancellation or payment failure']
  expired [note: 'Released by the sweeper after expires_at']
}

Enum movement_type {
  opening_balance [note: '+ initial stock at the start of the history']
  purchase_receipt [note: '+ goods received from a supplier']
  transfer_out [note: '- goods sent to another stock location']
  transfer_in [note: '+ goods received from another stock location']
  sale [note: '- goods issued for an order line at fulfilment']
  return_restock [note: '+ resellable returned goods put back into stock']
  stock_adjustment [note: '+/- stock count correction']
  write_off [note: '- damaged, lost or obsolete stock']
}

Enum payment_method {
  card
  mobile_payment [note: 'Swish, Vipps MobilePay']
  buy_now_pay_later [note: 'Invoice or instalments']
  cash [note: 'In-store orders only']
}

Enum payment_status {
  pending [note: 'Attempt created, provider result not known yet']
  authorized [note: 'Provider authorized; captured or voided under the order lock']
  captured
  failed
  voided [note: 'Authorization released because the order could no longer be confirmed']
}

Enum shipment_status {
  label_created
  dispatched
  in_transit
  delivered
  delivery_failed
}

Enum return_status {
  requested
  received [note: 'Goods received and inspected, refund amounts fixed']
  completed [note: 'Refund succeeded']
  rejected [note: 'No refund, no restock']
}

Enum return_reason {
  defective
  damaged_in_transit
  wrong_item
  not_as_described
  changed_mind
  other
}

Enum item_condition {
  resellable
  damaged
}

Enum refund_status {
  pending
  succeeded
  failed
}

Enum refund_reason {
  "return"
  order_cancellation
  goodwill
}

Enum change_operation {
  "insert"
  "update"
  "delete"
}

// =====================================================================
// Reference data and currency
// =====================================================================

Table currencies {
  currency_code char(3) [pk, note: 'ISO 4217: SEK, NOK, DKK, EUR']
  currency_name varchar(50) [not null]
  minor_units smallint [not null, default: 2]
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
}

Table countries {
  country_code char(2) [pk, note: 'ISO 3166-1 alpha-2: SE, NO, DK, FI']
  country_name varchar(100) [not null, unique]
  currency_code char(3) [not null, note: 'Current local currency. Transactions keep their own currency_code, so history survives a currency change.']
  vat_rate numeric(5,4) [not null, note: 'Current standard VAT rate, e.g. 0.2500. Order lines snapshot the rate used.']
  time_zone varchar(64) [not null, note: 'IANA zone, e.g. Europe/Stockholm, used for local reporting dates']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  checks {
    `vat_rate >= 0 AND vat_rate < 1` [name: 'ck_countries_vat_rate']
  }
}

Table exchange_rates {
  exchange_rate_id bigint [pk, increment]
  rate_date date [not null]
  base_currency_code char(3) [not null, default: 'EUR']
  quote_currency_code char(3) [not null]
  rate numeric(18,8) [not null, note: 'Units of quote currency per 1 unit of base currency']
  source varchar(50) [not null, default: 'synthetic_ecb']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (base_currency_code, quote_currency_code, rate_date) [unique, name: 'uq_exchange_rates_pair_date']
  }

  checks {
    `rate > 0` [name: 'ck_exchange_rates_rate_positive']
    `base_currency_code <> quote_currency_code` [name: 'ck_exchange_rates_distinct_pair']
  }

  Note: '''
Insert-only daily history, one row per currency pair per calendar day (EUR base, ECB style) [PG insert-only guard].
Rates are never updated, so every historical conversion is reproducible. amount_eur = amount / rate for the event date; EUR amounts need no conversion.
'''
}

// =====================================================================
// Customers, catalogue and promotions
// =====================================================================

Table customers {
  customer_id bigint [pk, increment]
  customer_number varchar(20) [not null, unique, note: 'Stable business key, e.g. C00012345']
  first_name varchar(100) [not null]
  last_name varchar(100) [not null]
  email varchar(255) [not null, unique]
  phone varchar(30)
  birth_date date
  address_line1 varchar(200)
  postal_code varchar(10)
  city varchar(100)
  country_code char(2) [not null]
  signup_channel sales_channel [not null]
  registered_at timestamptz [not null]
  is_active boolean [not null, default: true, note: 'Deactivation instead of hard delete']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    country_code [name: 'ix_customers_country_code']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_customers_updated_at']
  }
}

Table product_categories {
  category_id bigint [pk, increment]
  category_code varchar(30) [not null, unique, note: 'e.g. LAPTOPS, TV_AUDIO']
  category_name varchar(100) [not null, unique]
  is_active boolean [not null, default: true]
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
}

Table products {
  product_id bigint [pk, increment]
  sku varchar(32) [not null, unique, note: 'Stable business key']
  product_name varchar(200) [not null]
  brand varchar(100) [not null]
  category_id bigint [not null]
  launched_on date
  discontinued_on date
  is_active boolean [not null, default: true]
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    category_id [name: 'ix_products_category_id']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_products_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `discontinued_on IS NULL OR launched_on IS NULL OR discontinued_on >= launched_on` [name: 'ck_products_dates']
  }
}

Table product_prices {
  product_price_id bigint [pk, increment]
  product_id bigint [not null]
  country_code char(2) [not null, note: 'Sales market of this price']
  currency_code char(3) [not null, note: 'Equals the market currency when the price was set']
  list_price numeric(12,2) [not null, note: 'Consumer unit price incl. VAT, local currency']
  unit_cost numeric(12,2) [not null, note: 'Landed unit cost excl. VAT, local currency']
  valid_from timestamptz [not null]
  valid_to timestamptz [note: 'Exclusive end. NULL means current price.']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (product_id, country_code, valid_from) [unique, name: 'uq_product_prices_product_country_from']
    country_code [name: 'ix_product_prices_country_code']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_product_prices_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `list_price > 0` [name: 'ck_product_prices_list_price']
    `unit_cost >= 0` [name: 'ck_product_prices_unit_cost']
    `valid_to IS NULL OR valid_to > valid_from` [name: 'ck_product_prices_validity']
  }

  Note: '''
Effective-dated price list per product and market.
[PG] No overlapping validity per product and country: EXCLUDE USING gist (product_id WITH =, country_code WITH =, tstzrange(valid_from, valid_to) WITH &&), requires btree_gist.
Order lines copy list_price and unit_cost at purchase time, so later price changes never alter past sales.
'''
}

Table promotions {
  promotion_id bigint [pk, increment]
  promotion_code varchar(30) [not null, unique, note: 'e.g. BLACKFRIDAY-2025']
  promotion_name varchar(100) [not null]
  discount_percent numeric(5,2) [not null]
  category_id bigint [note: 'NULL means all categories']
  country_code char(2) [note: 'NULL means all markets']
  channel sales_channel [note: 'NULL means online and in store']
  starts_at timestamptz [not null]
  ends_at timestamptz [not null]
  is_active boolean [not null, default: true]
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (starts_at, ends_at) [name: 'ix_promotions_window']
    category_id [name: 'ix_promotions_category_id']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_promotions_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `discount_percent > 0 AND discount_percent < 100` [name: 'ck_promotions_discount_percent']
    `ends_at > starts_at` [name: 'ck_promotions_window']
  }

  Note: '''
Seasonal percentage discounts per order line, at most one promotion per line. Phase 2.
[APP] Eligibility: promotion active, placed_at inside the window, and category, order country and channel match.
The applied percentage is copied to order_items.discount_percent, so later edits to a promotion never change past sales.
'''
}

// =====================================================================
// Stock locations and inventory
// =====================================================================

Table stock_locations {
  stock_location_id bigint [pk, increment]
  location_code varchar(20) [not null, unique, note: 'Stable business key, e.g. SE-STO-01, SE-WH-01']
  location_name varchar(100) [not null]
  location_type stock_location_type [not null]
  country_code char(2) [not null]
  address_line1 varchar(200) [not null]
  postal_code varchar(10) [not null]
  city varchar(100) [not null]
  opened_on date [not null]
  is_active boolean [not null, default: true]
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (stock_location_id, location_type) [unique, name: 'uq_stock_locations_id_type', note: 'Target of the type-checked FKs from stores and warehouses']
    country_code [name: 'ix_stock_locations_country_code']
  }

  Note: '''
Supertype for every place that holds stock (12 stores and 4 warehouses). All stock tables reference it with ordinary FKs; there is no polymorphic location_id plus location_type pair.
[DBML] Each store or warehouse row points to exactly one location of its own type through the composite FK on (stock_location_id, location_type).
[PG] Deferred constraint trigger: every location has its matching subtype row at commit (both rows are inserted in one transaction).
'''
}

Table stores {
  store_id bigint [pk, increment]
  stock_location_id bigint [not null]
  location_type stock_location_type [not null, default: 'store', note: 'Fixed discriminator used by the composite FK']
  store_format varchar(20) [not null, default: 'standard', note: 'flagship or standard']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (stock_location_id, location_type) [unique, name: 'uq_stores_stock_location', note: 'One store per stock location (location_type is fixed)']
    (store_id, stock_location_id) [unique, name: 'uq_stores_store_location', note: 'Target of the in-store fulfilment FK from orders']
  }

  checks {
    `location_type = 'store'` [name: 'ck_stores_location_type']
  }
}

Table warehouses {
  warehouse_id bigint [pk, increment]
  stock_location_id bigint [not null]
  location_type stock_location_type [not null, default: 'warehouse', note: 'Fixed discriminator used by the composite FK']
  ships_online_orders boolean [not null, default: true]
  storage_capacity_units integer
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (stock_location_id, location_type) [unique, name: 'uq_warehouses_stock_location', note: 'One warehouse per stock location (location_type is fixed)']
  }

  checks {
    `location_type = 'warehouse'` [name: 'ck_warehouses_location_type']
    `storage_capacity_units IS NULL OR storage_capacity_units > 0` [name: 'ck_warehouses_capacity']
  }
}

Table inventory_balances {
  inventory_balance_id bigint [pk, increment]
  stock_location_id bigint [not null]
  product_id bigint [not null]
  quantity_on_hand integer [not null, default: 0, note: 'Maintained only by trigger = SUM(inventory_movements.quantity_change)']
  quantity_reserved integer [not null, default: 0, note: 'Maintained only by trigger = SUM(quantity) of active reservations']
  reorder_point integer [not null, default: 0, note: 'Replenishment trigger level, set by the application']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (stock_location_id, product_id) [unique, name: 'uq_inventory_balances_location_product']
    product_id [name: 'ix_inventory_balances_product_id']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_inventory_balances_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `quantity_on_hand >= 0` [name: 'ck_inventory_balances_on_hand']
    `quantity_reserved >= 0` [name: 'ck_inventory_balances_reserved']
    `quantity_reserved <= quantity_on_hand` [name: 'ck_inventory_balances_no_oversell']
    `reorder_point >= 0` [name: 'ck_inventory_balances_reorder_point']
  }

  Note: '''
Current stock position, one row per location and product, created with zero quantities when the product is listed at the location.
Available to sell = quantity_on_hand - quantity_reserved (derived, not stored).
[DBML] The no_oversell CHECK is the final guard: concurrent updates of the same row are serialized by its row lock and the CHECK is evaluated on the latest version, so the losing transaction fails with 23514 instead of overselling. It also blocks transfers and write-offs of reserved units.
[PG] Quantities change only through triggers: AFTER INSERT on inventory_movements adds quantity_change to quantity_on_hand; AFTER INSERT or UPDATE OF status on inventory_reservations adds the quantity when a row becomes active and subtracts it when it leaves active. The application role has no UPDATE privilege on the two quantity columns. This guarantees on_hand = SUM(movements) and reserved = SUM(active reservations).
[APP] Units of work U2 and U5 lock these rows FOR UPDATE in (stock_location_id, product_id) order before changing anything (see project note).
'''
}

Table inventory_reservations {
  reservation_id bigint [pk, increment]
  order_item_id bigint [not null]
  stock_location_id bigint [not null]
  product_id bigint [not null]
  quantity integer [not null]
  status reservation_status [not null, default: 'active']
  reserved_at timestamptz [not null]
  expires_at timestamptz [not null, note: 'APP parameter: reserved_at + 15 min online, + 5 min in store']
  closed_at timestamptz [note: 'When consumed, released or expired']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    order_item_id [name: 'ix_inventory_reservations_order_item_id']
    (stock_location_id, product_id, status) [name: 'ix_inventory_reservations_location_product_status']
    (status, expires_at) [name: 'ix_inventory_reservations_status_expires_at', note: 'Expiry sweeper']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_inventory_reservations_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `quantity > 0` [name: 'ck_inventory_reservations_quantity']
    `expires_at > reserved_at` [name: 'ck_inventory_reservations_expiry']
    `(status = 'active') = (closed_at IS NULL)` [name: 'ck_inventory_reservations_closed_at']
  }

  Note: '''
One reservation per order line at the order fulfilment location, created in unit of work U2 (all lines or none).
[PG] Partial unique index on (order_item_id) WHERE status = active: a retried U2 cannot reserve the same line twice.
[PG] Trigger: stock_location_id = orders.fulfilment_location_id, product_id and quantity equal the order line; only active -> consumed, released or expired; closed rows are immutable; each transition updates inventory_balances.quantity_reserved.
Lifecycle: consumed in U5 (fulfilment), released in U3 (final payment failure) or cancellation, expired in U4 (sweeper).
'''
}

Table inventory_movements {
  movement_id bigint [pk, increment]
  stock_location_id bigint [not null]
  product_id bigint [not null]
  movement_type movement_type [not null]
  quantity_change integer [not null, note: 'Signed: positive adds stock, negative removes stock']
  occurred_at timestamptz [not null, note: 'Business time of the physical movement']
  order_item_id bigint [note: 'Required for sale']
  return_item_id bigint [note: 'Required for return_restock (Phase 4)']
  related_movement_id bigint [note: 'transfer_in points to its matching transfer_out']
  external_reference varchar(64) [note: 'Supplier delivery note or stock count document']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (stock_location_id, product_id, occurred_at) [name: 'ix_inventory_movements_location_product_time']
    order_item_id [name: 'ix_inventory_movements_order_item_id']
    return_item_id [name: 'ix_inventory_movements_return_item_id']
    related_movement_id [name: 'ix_inventory_movements_related_movement_id']
    created_at [name: 'ix_inventory_movements_created_at']
  }

  checks {
    `quantity_change <> 0` [name: 'ck_inventory_movements_nonzero']
    `movement_type NOT IN ('opening_balance', 'purchase_receipt', 'transfer_in', 'return_restock') OR quantity_change > 0` [name: 'ck_inventory_movements_inbound_positive']
    `movement_type NOT IN ('transfer_out', 'sale', 'write_off') OR quantity_change < 0` [name: 'ck_inventory_movements_outbound_negative']
    `(movement_type = 'sale') = (order_item_id IS NOT NULL)` [name: 'ck_inventory_movements_sale_ref']
    `(movement_type = 'return_restock') = (return_item_id IS NOT NULL)` [name: 'ck_inventory_movements_restock_ref']
    `(movement_type = 'transfer_in') = (related_movement_id IS NOT NULL)` [name: 'ck_inventory_movements_transfer_ref']
  }

  Note: '''
Insert-only stock ledger; corrections are new stock_adjustment rows [PG insert-only guard and privileges].
Reservations never create movements. Stock leaves on hand exactly once, through the sale movement in U5, so nothing is counted twice.
[APP] In U5, consume the reservation BEFORE inserting the sale movement, so reserved <= on_hand holds after every statement.
[PG] Partial unique indexes: (order_item_id) WHERE movement_type = sale; (return_item_id) WHERE movement_type = return_restock; (related_movement_id) WHERE movement_type = transfer_in. A retried step therefore fails with 23505 instead of moving stock twice.
[PG] Trigger: a sale needs a consumed reservation for the same order line, location and quantity; a transfer_in needs a transfer_out of the same product at another location with the opposite quantity; a return_restock needs a resellable return item received at this location.
'''
}

// =====================================================================
// Orders and payments
// =====================================================================

Table orders {
  order_id bigint [pk, increment]
  order_number varchar(30) [not null, unique, note: 'Stable business key, e.g. SE-2025-00012345']
  customer_id bigint [note: 'Required for online orders. NULL allowed for anonymous in-store sales.']
  channel sales_channel [not null]
  store_id bigint [note: 'Selling store. Required for in_store, NULL for online.']
  fulfilment_location_id bigint [not null, note: 'Stock location that reserves and issues the stock']
  country_code char(2) [not null, note: 'Sales market: drives price list, VAT and currency']
  currency_code char(3) [not null, note: 'Currency of every amount on this order and its child records']
  status order_status [not null, default: 'placed']
  cancellation_reason varchar(50) [note: 'out_of_stock, reservation_expired or customer_request']
  items_total numeric(14,2) [not null, default: 0, note: 'SUM(order_items.line_total), incl. VAT']
  shipping_fee numeric(12,2) [not null, default: 0, note: 'Incl. VAT']
  shipping_vat_amount numeric(12,2) [not null, default: 0, note: 'round(shipping_fee * vat_rate / (1 + vat_rate), 2) at the line VAT rate']
  order_total numeric(14,2) [not null, default: 0, note: 'items_total + shipping_fee, the amount to pay']
  shipping_address_line1 varchar(200)
  shipping_postal_code varchar(10)
  shipping_city varchar(100)
  placed_at timestamptz [not null]
  confirmed_at timestamptz
  fulfilled_at timestamptz [note: 'Revenue recognition time. Picked and packed (online) or handed over (in store).']
  cancelled_at timestamptz [note: 'Also set for payment_failed']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    customer_id [name: 'ix_orders_customer_id']
    store_id [name: 'ix_orders_store_id']
    fulfilment_location_id [name: 'ix_orders_fulfilment_location_id']
    (status, placed_at) [name: 'ix_orders_status_placed_at']
    placed_at [name: 'ix_orders_placed_at']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_orders_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `(channel = 'in_store') = (store_id IS NOT NULL)` [name: 'ck_orders_store_matches_channel']
    `channel = 'in_store' OR customer_id IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_orders_online_customer']
    `channel = 'in_store' OR (shipping_address_line1 IS NOT NULL AND shipping_postal_code IS NOT NULL AND shipping_city IS NOT NULL)` [name: 'ck_orders_online_address']
    `channel = 'online' OR shipping_fee = 0` [name: 'ck_orders_in_store_no_shipping']
    `items_total >= 0 AND shipping_fee >= 0` [name: 'ck_orders_amounts_non_negative']
    `shipping_vat_amount >= 0 AND shipping_vat_amount <= shipping_fee` [name: 'ck_orders_shipping_vat']
    `order_total = items_total + shipping_fee` [name: 'ck_orders_total']
    `status NOT IN ('cancelled', 'payment_failed') OR cancelled_at IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_orders_cancelled_at']
    `status NOT IN ('confirmed', 'fulfilled') OR confirmed_at IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_orders_confirmed_at']
    `status <> 'fulfilled' OR fulfilled_at IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_orders_fulfilled_at']
  }

  Note: '''
Lifecycle: placed -> stock_reserved -> confirmed -> fulfilled, with exits to payment_failed or cancelled. Units of work U1 to U5 are in the project note.
[PG] Status transition trigger, allowed moves only: placed -> stock_reserved or cancelled; stock_reserved -> confirmed, payment_failed or cancelled; confirmed -> fulfilled (or cancelled once refunds exist, Phase 4); fulfilled and the exits are final.
[PG] Deferred constraint trigger: at commit items_total = SUM(order_items.line_total).
[DBML] In-store orders are fulfilled from the selling store own stock location via the composite FK (store_id, fulfilment_location_id) -> stores.
[APP] Online orders are fulfilled from the warehouse in their country and delivered within it. Post-sale events live in shipments, returns, return_items and refunds.
'''
}

Table order_items {
  order_item_id bigint [pk, increment]
  order_id bigint [not null]
  line_number smallint [not null]
  product_id bigint [not null]
  promotion_id bigint [note: 'Phase 2; NULL in the MVP']
  quantity integer [not null]
  unit_price numeric(12,2) [not null, note: 'Regular unit price incl. VAT at purchase time, copied from product_prices']
  unit_cost numeric(12,2) [not null, note: 'Unit cost excl. VAT at purchase time, copied from product_prices']
  discount_percent numeric(5,2) [not null, default: 0, note: 'Percentage applied, copied from the promotion; 0 without promotion']
  discount_amount numeric(12,2) [not null, default: 0, note: 'round(quantity * unit_price * discount_percent / 100, 2)']
  line_total numeric(14,2) [not null, note: 'Net sales incl. VAT = quantity * unit_price - discount_amount']
  vat_rate numeric(5,4) [not null]
  vat_amount numeric(12,2) [not null, note: 'round(line_total * vat_rate / (1 + vat_rate), 2)']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (order_id, line_number) [unique, name: 'uq_order_items_order_line']
    (order_id, product_id) [unique, name: 'uq_order_items_order_product']
    product_id [name: 'ix_order_items_product_id']
    promotion_id [name: 'ix_order_items_promotion_id']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_order_items_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `quantity > 0` [name: 'ck_order_items_quantity']
    `unit_price >= 0 AND unit_cost >= 0` [name: 'ck_order_items_price_cost']
    `discount_percent >= 0 AND discount_percent < 100` [name: 'ck_order_items_discount_percent']
    `(promotion_id IS NULL) = (discount_percent = 0)` [name: 'ck_order_items_promotion_discount']
    `discount_amount = round(quantity * unit_price * discount_percent / 100, 2)` [name: 'ck_order_items_discount_rounding']
    `line_total = quantity * unit_price - discount_amount` [name: 'ck_order_items_line_total']
    `vat_rate >= 0 AND vat_rate < 1` [name: 'ck_order_items_vat_rate']
    `vat_amount = round(line_total * vat_rate / (1 + vat_rate), 2)` [name: 'ck_order_items_vat_rounding']
  }

  Note: '''
Purchase-time snapshot: price, cost, discount percentage and VAT rate are copied when the order is placed and never recalculated.
[DBML] The discount, line total and VAT CHECKs make the Power BI formulas in the project note hold row by row, with round half away from zero.
Derived per line (not stored): net_revenue_excl_vat = line_total - vat_amount; cogs = quantity * unit_cost; gross_profit = net_revenue_excl_vat - cogs.
[PG] Trigger: discount_percent equals the promotion percentage; vat_rate equals the order country rate at placed_at; lines are immutable after the order leaves placed.
'''
}

Table payment_attempts {
  payment_attempt_id bigint [pk, increment]
  order_id bigint [not null]
  attempt_number smallint [not null]
  payment_method payment_method [not null]
  payment_provider varchar(50) [not null, note: 'Synthetic payment service provider']
  amount numeric(14,2) [not null, note: 'In orders.currency_code, equals order_total']
  status payment_status [not null, default: 'pending']
  failure_reason varchar(50) [note: 'card_declined, insufficient_funds, timeout']
  provider_reference varchar(64) [unique, note: 'Provider transaction id']
  attempted_at timestamptz [not null]
  authorized_at timestamptz
  captured_at timestamptz
  failed_at timestamptz
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (order_id, attempt_number) [unique, name: 'uq_payment_attempts_order_attempt']
    (status, attempted_at) [name: 'ix_payment_attempts_status_attempted_at']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_payment_attempts_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `attempt_number > 0` [name: 'ck_payment_attempts_attempt_number']
    `amount > 0` [name: 'ck_payment_attempts_amount']
    `status <> 'failed' OR (failed_at IS NOT NULL AND failure_reason IS NOT NULL)` [name: 'ck_payment_attempts_failed']
    `status NOT IN ('authorized', 'captured', 'voided') OR authorized_at IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_payment_attempts_authorized_at']
    `status <> 'captured' OR captured_at IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_payment_attempts_captured_at']
  }

  Note: '''
One row per attempt (unit of work U3). Idempotency key sent to the provider: order_number-attempt_number.
[PG] Partial unique index on (order_id) WHERE status IN (pending, authorized, captured): at most one live attempt per order, so a new attempt is possible only after a failure or void, and an order can never be captured twice.
[PG] Trigger: amount = orders.order_total; cash only for in_store orders; transitions pending -> authorized or failed, authorized -> captured or voided; capture only while the order is stock_reserved with all reservations active (order row locked).
[APP] Maximum 3 attempts per order. After the last failure, or when reservations expire, the order becomes payment_failed or cancelled and its reservations are released in the same transaction.
'''
}

// =====================================================================
// Fulfilment and after-sales
// =====================================================================

Table shipments {
  shipment_id bigint [pk, increment]
  order_id bigint [not null, unique]
  carrier varchar(50) [not null]
  tracking_number varchar(64) [unique]
  status shipment_status [not null, default: 'label_created']
  estimated_delivery_date date
  dispatched_at timestamptz
  delivered_at timestamptz
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (status, dispatched_at) [name: 'ix_shipments_status_dispatched_at']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_shipments_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `delivered_at IS NULL OR (dispatched_at IS NOT NULL AND delivered_at >= dispatched_at)` [name: 'ck_shipments_delivery_after_dispatch']
    `status <> 'delivered' OR delivered_at IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_shipments_delivered_at']
  }

  Note: '''
Phase 3. One shipment per online order [DBML unique order_id].
[PG] Trigger: only for online orders with status fulfilled. Ships from orders.fulfilment_location_id to the order shipping address. In-store orders have no shipment.
'''
}

Table returns {
  return_id bigint [pk, increment]
  return_number varchar(30) [not null, unique, note: 'Stable business key; also the refund_reference of its refund']
  order_id bigint [not null]
  received_location_id bigint [not null, note: 'Store or warehouse that receives the goods']
  status return_status [not null, default: 'requested']
  requested_at timestamptz [not null]
  received_at timestamptz
  closed_at timestamptz [note: 'Completed or rejected. Reporting date of returned revenue.']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    order_id [name: 'ix_returns_order_id']
    received_location_id [name: 'ix_returns_received_location_id']
    (status, requested_at) [name: 'ix_returns_status_requested_at']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_returns_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `received_at IS NULL OR received_at >= requested_at` [name: 'ck_returns_received_after_requested']
    `status NOT IN ('received', 'completed') OR received_at IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_returns_received_at']
    `status NOT IN ('completed', 'rejected') OR closed_at IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_returns_closed_at']
  }

  Note: '''
Phase 4. An order can have several returns (partial returns).
Lifecycle: requested -> received (goods inspected, item_condition and refund amounts fixed, restock movements written) -> completed (in the same transaction that marks its refund succeeded); or requested/received -> rejected (no refund, no restock).
[APP] Allowed only for fulfilled in-store orders or delivered online orders, within the return window. Shipping fees are not refunded on returns (assumption).
'''
}

Table return_items {
  return_item_id bigint [pk, increment]
  return_id bigint [not null]
  order_item_id bigint [not null]
  quantity integer [not null]
  reason return_reason [not null]
  item_condition item_condition [note: 'Set on inspection, NULL until received']
  refund_amount numeric(12,2) [not null, default: 0, note: 'Incl. VAT, cumulative-difference rule']
  refund_vat_amount numeric(12,2) [not null, default: 0, note: 'VAT inside refund_amount, same rule applied to vat_amount']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (return_id, order_item_id) [unique, name: 'uq_return_items_return_order_item']
    order_item_id [name: 'ix_return_items_order_item_id']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_return_items_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `quantity > 0` [name: 'ck_return_items_quantity']
    `refund_amount >= 0` [name: 'ck_return_items_refund_amount']
    `refund_vat_amount >= 0 AND refund_vat_amount <= refund_amount` [name: 'ck_return_items_refund_vat']
  }

  Note: '''
Refund amounts use a cumulative-difference rule so that partial returns add up exactly. For an order line with quantity N, line_total L and vat_amount V, let C_before be the units already returned on earlier non-rejected return items of that line (by return_item_id) and C_after = C_before + quantity:
refund_amount = round(L * C_after / N, 2) - round(L * C_before / N, 2)
refund_vat_amount = round(V * C_after / N, 2) - round(V * C_before / N, 2)
When all N units are back, the refunds sum to exactly L and V, with no leftover cent.
[PG] Trigger (BEFORE INSERT or UPDATE): lock the order_items row FOR UPDATE, then check the line belongs to the return order, C_after <= N, and the two amounts follow the rule. The row lock serializes concurrent returns of the same line, which prevents over-returning.
Resellable items get one return_restock movement when the return is received (unit of work U6). Damaged items are not restocked.
'''
}

Table refunds {
  refund_id bigint [pk, increment]
  refund_reference varchar(40) [not null, note: 'Refund obligation key shared by all its attempts: return_number, CANCEL-order_number or GW-order_number-n']
  attempt_number smallint [not null]
  payment_attempt_id bigint [not null, note: 'The captured payment being refunded']
  return_id bigint [note: 'Set when the refund settles a return. NULL for cancellations and goodwill.']
  reason refund_reason [not null]
  amount numeric(14,2) [not null, note: 'Incl. VAT, in orders.currency_code']
  status refund_status [not null, default: 'pending']
  failure_reason varchar(50)
  provider_reference varchar(64) [unique]
  requested_at timestamptz [not null]
  processed_at timestamptz
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]

  indexes {
    (refund_reference, attempt_number) [unique, name: 'uq_refunds_reference_attempt']
    payment_attempt_id [name: 'ix_refunds_payment_attempt_id']
    return_id [name: 'ix_refunds_return_id']
    (status, requested_at) [name: 'ix_refunds_status_requested_at']
    updated_at [name: 'ix_refunds_updated_at']
  }

  checks {
    `attempt_number > 0` [name: 'ck_refunds_attempt_number']
    `amount > 0` [name: 'ck_refunds_amount']
    `(reason = 'return') = (return_id IS NOT NULL)` [name: 'ck_refunds_return_ref']
    `status = 'pending' OR processed_at IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_refunds_processed_at']
    `status <> 'failed' OR failure_reason IS NOT NULL` [name: 'ck_refunds_failure_reason']
  }

  Note: '''
Phase 4. One row per refund attempt; retries of the same obligation share refund_reference with attempt_number 1, 2, 3. Idempotency key sent to the provider: refund_reference-attempt_number.
[PG] Partial unique index on (refund_reference) WHERE status IN (pending, succeeded): a retry is possible only after the previous attempt failed, and a succeeded obligation can never be paid twice.
[PG] Trigger (BEFORE INSERT and BEFORE UPDATE OF amount, status): lock the payment_attempts row FOR UPDATE, require status captured, and require SUM(amount) of its pending and succeeded refunds, this row included, <= the captured amount. Counting pending rows means two concurrent refunds cannot both pass. For reason return: the return belongs to the same order, has status received, and amount = SUM(return_items.refund_amount). For order_cancellation: the order is confirmed and not fulfilled, and amount = order_total.
[APP] A timed-out call stays pending and is reconciled with the provider before any new attempt. A succeeded return refund sets its return completed in the same transaction.
Reconciliation checks for the pipeline: per completed return, succeeded refund = SUM(return_items.refund_amount); per order line, returned units <= quantity and refunds <= line_total; per payment, succeeded refunds <= captured amount; net collected = captured amount - succeeded refunds.
'''
}

// =====================================================================
// Change data capture
// =====================================================================

Table change_log {
  change_id bigint [pk, increment, note: 'PG: identity with CACHE 1. Orders changes to the same record; not a commit-order watermark.']
  table_name varchar(63) [not null]
  record_key varchar(64) [not null, note: 'Primary key value of the changed row, as text']
  operation change_operation [not null]
  row_data jsonb [not null, note: 'Row image after the change, or the deleted row for delete']
  transaction_id xid8 [not null, default: `pg_current_xact_id()`, note: 'Top-level transaction id of the writer; drives the snapshot checkpoint']
  changed_at timestamptz [not null, default: `clock_timestamp()`, note: 'Informational only']

  indexes {
    transaction_id [name: 'ix_change_log_transaction_id']
    (table_name, change_id) [name: 'ix_change_log_table_change_id']
  }

  Note: '''
Transactional change log (outbox pattern): one generic AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE row trigger on every operational table writes here in the same transaction, so a committed change is never missed and a rolled-back one never appears [PG].
Why not change_id or time watermarks: identity values and now() are assigned when a transaction writes, not when it commits. A transaction that writes first and commits last can land below a watermark that was already passed, and no fixed overlap window bounds how long a transaction stays open.
Safe extraction run (consumer row in change_log_checkpoints):
1. BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ READ ONLY; read S_new = pg_current_snapshot() as the first statement.
2. Read change_log rows WHERE transaction_id >= pg_snapshot_xmin(S_prev) AND NOT pg_visible_in_snapshot(transaction_id, S_prev), ORDER BY change_id. Under S_new this returns exactly the transactions that committed after S_prev and before S_new; still-running ones appear in a later run.
3. COMMIT the read, load the batch into the landing zone idempotently (unique on change_id), commit the destination.
4. Only then set change_log_checkpoints.last_snapshot = S_new. A crash before step 4 repeats the batch, which the idempotent load absorbs.
Merging: keep the row with the highest change_id per (table_name, record_key). This is safe because writers to the same row are serialized by its row lock, so a later committed change always gets a higher change_id, provided the identity uses CACHE 1.
Bootstrap: take S0 and copy every tracked table inside one REPEATABLE READ transaction, then store S0 as the first checkpoint.
Purge: delete rows WHERE transaction_id < MIN(pg_snapshot_xmin(last_snapshot)) over all consumers; those rows are loaded by everyone.
Requirements [PG]: PostgreSQL 13 or later (xid8, pg_current_xact_id, pg_current_snapshot, pg_visible_in_snapshot); record pg_current_xact_id(), never the row xmin; insert-only; not tracked itself; idle_in_transaction_session_timeout so a forgotten transaction cannot hold back the snapshot xmin; monitor the oldest open transaction.
'''
}

Table change_log_checkpoints {
  consumer_name varchar(50) [pk, note: 'Extraction consumer, e.g. nordiccart_elt']
  last_snapshot pg_snapshot [not null, note: 'Snapshot of the last batch committed in the destination']
  created_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`]
  updated_at timestamptz [not null, default: `now()`, note: 'Time of the last checkpoint']

  Note: '''
One row per extraction consumer. Written only by the extractor role after its destination load has committed (step 4 in change_log).
Its xmin, pg_snapshot_xmin(last_snapshot), is the lower bound for the next run and for the change_log purge. Not tracked in change_log.
'''
}

// =====================================================================
// Relationships
// =====================================================================

// Reference data
Ref fk_countries_currency: countries.currency_code > currencies.currency_code
Ref fk_exchange_rates_base_currency: exchange_rates.base_currency_code > currencies.currency_code
Ref fk_exchange_rates_quote_currency: exchange_rates.quote_currency_code > currencies.currency_code

// Customers, catalogue and promotions
Ref fk_customers_country: customers.country_code > countries.country_code
Ref fk_products_category: products.category_id > product_categories.category_id
Ref fk_product_prices_product: product_prices.product_id > products.product_id
Ref fk_product_prices_country: product_prices.country_code > countries.country_code
Ref fk_product_prices_currency: product_prices.currency_code > currencies.currency_code
Ref fk_promotions_category: promotions.category_id >? product_categories.category_id
Ref fk_promotions_country: promotions.country_code >? countries.country_code

// Stock locations (type-checked supertype and subtypes)
Ref fk_stock_locations_country: stock_locations.country_code > countries.country_code
Ref fk_stores_stock_location: stock_locations.(stock_location_id, location_type) - stores.(stock_location_id, location_type)
Ref fk_warehouses_stock_location: stock_locations.(stock_location_id, location_type) - warehouses.(stock_location_id, location_type)

// Inventory
Ref fk_inventory_balances_location: inventory_balances.stock_location_id > stock_locations.stock_location_id
Ref fk_inventory_balances_product: inventory_balances.product_id > products.product_id
Ref fk_inventory_reservations_balance: inventory_reservations.(stock_location_id, product_id) > inventory_balances.(stock_location_id, product_id)
Ref fk_inventory_reservations_order_item: inventory_reservations.order_item_id > order_items.order_item_id
Ref fk_inventory_movements_balance: inventory_movements.(stock_location_id, product_id) > inventory_balances.(stock_location_id, product_id)
Ref fk_inventory_movements_order_item: inventory_movements.order_item_id >? order_items.order_item_id
Ref fk_inventory_movements_return_item: inventory_movements.return_item_id >? return_items.return_item_id
Ref fk_inventory_movements_related: inventory_movements.related_movement_id >? inventory_movements.movement_id

// Orders and payments
Ref fk_orders_customer: orders.customer_id >? customers.customer_id
Ref fk_orders_store_fulfilment: orders.(store_id, fulfilment_location_id) >? stores.(store_id, stock_location_id)
Ref fk_orders_fulfilment_location: orders.fulfilment_location_id > stock_locations.stock_location_id
Ref fk_orders_country: orders.country_code > countries.country_code
Ref fk_orders_currency: orders.currency_code > currencies.currency_code
Ref fk_order_items_order: order_items.order_id > orders.order_id
Ref fk_order_items_product: order_items.product_id > products.product_id
Ref fk_order_items_promotion: order_items.promotion_id >? promotions.promotion_id
Ref fk_payment_attempts_order: payment_attempts.order_id > orders.order_id

// Fulfilment and after-sales
Ref fk_shipments_order: orders.order_id - shipments.order_id
Ref fk_returns_order: returns.order_id > orders.order_id
Ref fk_returns_received_location: returns.received_location_id > stock_locations.stock_location_id
Ref fk_return_items_return: return_items.return_id > returns.return_id
Ref fk_return_items_order_item: return_items.order_item_id > order_items.order_item_id
Ref fk_refunds_payment_attempt: refunds.payment_attempt_id > payment_attempts.payment_attempt_id
Ref fk_refunds_return: refunds.return_id >? returns.return_id
